  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nA tooling fix; no mathematical result, supplier or \u03ba change.\n\n**The defect:** `scripts/find_overlap.py --task` adds each research starting point's immediate parent folder as an OR search term. For nested files that folder is a generic word. The revision-4 `joint-query-controller` card searches for `evidence` (twice) and never for its package, `joint-query`; `centre-path-sharing` adds `proofs`. Generic hits can push a genuine lead below the default 20-result limit.\n\n**The fix:** take the package component of repository-relative `research/PACKAGE/...` paths, so a nested file contributes its package; top-level research documents contribute no term. Explicit and catalogue overlap terms (generic words included), snapshot collection, scoring, `--path` matching and coverage reporting are unchanged. The other revision-4 cards derive the same term sets as before.\n\n**Effect on one fixed snapshot** (403 open PRs and 810 Discussion records, plus this base's local records): `--task joint-query-controller` goes from 1,487 possible matches to 33, and `--task centre-path-sharing` from 386 to 261. The removed matches came only from the generic terms; every match to a retained term remains. These counts illustrate the defect; they are not test assertions or a novelty guarantee.\n\n**Remaining:** literal substring search is still heuristic, so this does not make overlap detection complete. Windows execution and hosted CI were not run.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Task ID / scope:** `overlap-receipt` (lookup key `5ae821d1b52092164092`), changed to `find_overlap/task-derived-terms/nested-research-package-roots`. Chooser nonce `062c8d19991bc9ca650f6c96f1b1d3b6`, track bit-mechanisms, priority revision 4; its suggestions (`bit-helper-contract`, `bit-new-register-model`) overlap this account's completed work, and the central handoffs considered were already claimed. The defect showed up while running overlap checks for those handoffs. No reroll.\n- **Base and head:** base `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`, rechecked unchanged at 21:12 UTC; contribution `5984d9999bceaeb7d1ce9a3f4edfbfcd0b70c118`. No pending dependency. Nick Petalas's earlier pagination repair (`9c2fdd89e553751e40ef8011716a249e8385d284`) is retained unchanged.\n- **Scope status:** changed, from an overlap-receipt investigation to a demonstrated coordination-tool defect that hides leads on a central card. It supports task coordination; it is not mathematical progress.\n- **Nearest work:** the existing pagination, Unicode and path-index tests (including PR #1702's fixture) cover other helper behaviour. The changed-file index and a live check of newer PRs show no open PR touching `scripts/find_overlap.py`.\n- **Claim:** [discussion #1200](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1200#discussioncomment-18934067).\n\n## Validation\n\n```sh\npython3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_research_overlap_terms.py'\npython3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_research_*.py'\ngit diff --check\n```\n\nLinux x86_64, CPython 3.12.3. The four new CLI regression groups (a lead displaced below the default limit, exclusively nested packages, explicit generic terms and `--all-terms` preserved, catalogue terms not treated as stopwords) use isolated offline snapshots and check that the cache bytes and coverage metadata are unchanged.\n- All four fail when run against the current helper on main, and pass with the fix.\n- The full research-workflow suite passes: 69 tests in about 6 seconds, rerun before submission under an 8 GiB memory cap. The suite's intentional failing-subprocess fixtures print tracebacks; those are passing negative controls.\n\n## Credit\n\nDouglas Colkitt and the contributors to the overlap helper and its tests, including Nick Petalas's pagination repair. Apache-2.0.\n\n**AI assistance:** submitted by @commdata2338 with substantial AI assistance. OpenAI Codex (GPT-6 Astra) found the defect and wrote the fix, the tests and the evidence; it was packaged and submitted with Claude Code. No independent human review is claimed.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nNone justified beyond this fix. A broader change to ranking would need its own relevance fixtures and overlap check.\n\n## Workflow feedback (optional)\n\n- **Friction:** the chooser's draw was lost to display truncation and recovered from the session log. Saving its JSON before rendering, for example with an `--output` path as suggested in PR #2048, would keep the first draw durable.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01X86sCSXdoDd61SDFfC1PKG\n",
